 },
 "model": "eleven_multilingual_v2",
 "generated": "2026-09-25T16:17:19.447Z",
 "files": {
  "1958": "scene2/audio/voice/585ae918f52ca423.mp3",
  "1960": "scene2/audio/voice/e209306e374d3bc1.mp3",
  "1962": "scene2/audio/voice/8837141ef4e81723.mp3",
  "1964": "scene2/audio/voice/f320459e1db13864.mp3",
  "1972": "scene2/audio/voice/fd63a4ca578b00a8.mp3",
  "1978": "scene2/audio/voice/514110d92496d655.mp3",
  "Looking up": "scene1/audio/voice/689bc2c96de76b7b.mp3",
  "For thousands of years, people looked up at the night sky and wondered what was out there. In the 1950s, they finally built rockets powerful enough to find out!": "scene1/audio/voice/3f53bdd3a2c725ef.mp3",
  "Sputnik 1": "scene1/audio/voice/bbcca7e163b7e5b2.mp3",
  "On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first human-made satellite. It was about the size of a beach ball, and it sent 'beep-beep' radio signals from space.": "scene1/audio/voice/eaa1fd246b19af9f.mp3",
  "NASA is born": "scene1/audio/voice/5bb5a7a7965362c1.mp3",
  "The United States created NASA, its space agency. NASA opened on October 1, 1958, to explore space for the good of everyone.": "scene1/audio/voice/3db61a21c7d604ca.mp3",
  "The first person in space": "scene1/audio/voice/f4454863b5f3bfdb.mp3",
  "On April 12, 1961, Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space. His ship, Vostok 1, went once around Earth in 108 minutes.": "scene1/audio/voice/ec709e4814dbbc55.mp3",
  "Cosmonaut or astronaut?": "scene1/audio/voice/369dcb87e02c6861.mp3",
  "'Cosmonaut' is the Russian word for a space traveler. Americans say 'astronaut', which comes from Greek words meaning 'star sailor'.": "scene1/audio/voice/61b5d5dac0236fda.mp3",
  "The Mercury Seven": "scene1/audio/voice/96a254f8af1f1d5c.mp3",
  "NASA's first program to fly people was called Project Mercury. In 1959, NASA chose seven pilots to be its very first astronauts.": "scene1/audio/voice/0ebbb2487083e44b.mp3",
  "Freedom 7": "scene1/audio/voice/10795a2f71afd2ae.mp3",
  "On May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard flew the Freedom 7 capsule and became the first American in space. Let's watch NASA's film of that day!": "scene1/audio/voice/8a73b2bd5b47b2a9.mp3",
  "Your mission": "scene1/audio/voice/e325e568dcbf6811.mp3",
  "Before we can fly, we need to understand how a spaceship works. Let's explore the 8 major systems every spaceship needs!": "scene1/audio/voice/6282923a9a8cde42.mp3",
  "The 8 major systems of a spaceship": "scene1/audio/voice/504c0dc87b5c9960.mp3",
  "Every spaceship is made of a few big systems that work together, like the parts of your body.": "scene1/audio/voice/6cb49aaf16f212bd.mp3",
  "Scroll through the 8 systems below. Drag the 3D model to spin it and look closely at the details!": "scene1/audio/voice/cbf7996be4a8d287.mp3",
  "Great job! You've explored all 8 systems. Now let's use what you learned to build Freedom 7.": "scene1/audio/voice/99834a0388274267.mp3",
  "Propulsion": "scene1/audio/voice/b8b88d45a1b9a4bc.mp3",
  "Burns fuel to push the ship up. Hot gas rushes out of the bottom and the rocket moves the other way, like a balloon letting out its air.": "scene1/audio/voice/071e01265c1904f3.mp3",
  "At the bottom of the rocket. Freedom 7 rode on a Redstone rocket with one big engine.": "scene1/audio/voice/d273c265b6b458a5.mp3",
  "Rockets carry their own oxygen, so their engines still work where there is no air!": "scene1/audio/voice/00f1f3cf4ffca376.mp3",
  "Guidance & Navigation": "scene1/audio/voice/a7275f46be669873.mp3",
  "Spinning gyroscopes and computers keep track of which way the ship is pointing and where it is going.": "scene1/audio/voice/35e81972e4004226.mp3",
  "Inside the rocket and the capsule. Apollo's guidance computer helped steer all the way to the Moon.": "scene1/audio/voice/5958fd6cda3eeaf0.mp3",
  "A spinning gyroscope tries to keep pointing the same way, like a spinning top that won't fall over.": "scene1/audio/voice/2fb261a5c3421e9a.mp3",
  "Attitude Control": "scene1/audio/voice/f61afc18ba707685.mp3",
  "Small thrusters puff out gas to turn the ship: nose up or down, left or right, or rolling around.": "scene1/audio/voice/e776e3f09d834904.mp3",
  "Around the outside of the capsule. Alan Shepard used a hand controller to fire them on Freedom 7.": "scene1/audio/voice/e65865dcb6c2b570.mp3",
  "In space, 'attitude' means the direction your spaceship is facing, not how you behave!": "scene1/audio/voice/434211aa131715fc.mp3",
  "Electrical Power": "scene1/audio/voice/a60c80838fc5f81f.mp3",
  "Gives electricity to the radios, lights, computers and fans.": "scene1/audio/voice/8304bd9fca491033.mp3",
  "Freedom 7 used batteries. Apollo used fuel cells, and satellites and space stations use solar panels.": "scene1/audio/voice/2f8415f0e8de2c32.mp3",
  "Apollo's fuel cells made electricity AND drinking water for the astronauts!": "scene1/audio/voice/517261f484a86563.mp3",
  "Communications": "scene1/audio/voice/b46be77d40a67ce4.mp3",
  "Radios and antennas send voices, pictures and data between the spaceship and people on Earth.": "scene1/audio/voice/0064fdc491c90184.mp3",
  "Antennas on the outside, radios inside. Giant dish antennas on Earth listen for the signals.": "scene1/audio/voice/8e0b8fd7f5cc8e09.mp3",
  "Radio signals travel at the speed of light. A message from the Moon reaches Earth in just over one second!": "scene1/audio/voice/2de22aaf255370c3.mp3",
  "Life Support": "scene1/audio/voice/e021e3cd9149afca.mp3",
  "Keeps astronauts alive: it gives them oxygen, removes the carbon dioxide they breathe out, and keeps the cabin at a safe pressure and temperature.": "scene1/audio/voice/b58f7566483ddb6e.mp3",
  "Inside the cabin and inside the astronaut's spacesuit.": "scene1/audio/voice/4a30874e1ba6fd3a.mp3",
  "Alan Shepard wore a pressure suit on Freedom 7, so he would stay safe even if the cabin lost its air.": "scene1/audio/voice/f9f0ae5a79070d08.mp3",
  "Heat Shield": "scene1/audio/voice/f50aece6b9270529.mp3",
  "Protects the capsule when it falls back into the air super fast. It squeezes the air in front of it so hard that the air glows hotter than lava!": "scene1/audio/voice/cc5652839ce0d9c5.mp3",
  "On the wide bottom of the capsule, which faces forward when it comes home.": "scene1/audio/voice/d63eaa7cde1b128a.mp3",
  "Some heat shields are made to slowly burn away on purpose, carrying the heat off with them.": "scene1/audio/voice/d6f8bbb2a6fe26dc.mp3",
  "Recovery & Landing": "scene1/audio/voice/bc68e70bcd827b9a.mp3",
  "Slows the capsule down so it can land gently.": "scene1/audio/voice/1c217c98e0a7600d.mp3",
  "Packed in the top of the capsule. Freedom 7 floated down under its parachute into the Atlantic Ocean.": "scene1/audio/voice/ca11e0d4e29dba7d.mp3",
  "A small parachute opens first to steady the capsule, then the big main parachute opens.": "scene1/audio/voice/6e118e51e5594e64.mp3",
  "Build Freedom 7": "scene1/audio/voice/b38f36b23b12f6a5.mp3",
  "Here is a big box of spaceship parts from many different missions.": "scene1/audio/voice/a44a6e331005e02b.mp3",
  "Freedom 7 needs exactly 6 of them. Follow the glowing arrow and tap the right parts to build it from the bottom up!": "scene1/audio/voice/45e8b485e838a65a.mp3",
  "Spaceship parts": "scene1/audio/voice/2583478e9f3701e0.mp3",
  "parts added": "scene1/audio/voice/49bbb9cf35dd6c08.mp3",
  "Not this one!": "scene1/audio/voice/bd4f4c17b6cd80e1.mp3",
  "You built Freedom 7! A Redstone rocket with a Mercury capsule on top. Let's take a closer look.": "scene1/audio/voice/b34b4c3db7931870.mp3",
  "Redstone Rocket": "scene1/audio/voice/c365ca1ad3e2cc77.mp3",
  "The Redstone rocket pushes Freedom 7 up into space. Its engine burned for about two and a half minutes.": "scene1/audio/voice/c57075f0fcf91471.mp3",
  "Retrorocket Pack": "scene1/audio/voice/52012174ea496f93.mp3",
  "Three small rockets strapped under the heat shield. They slow a capsule down to come home from orbit. Alan Shepard fired them as a test!": "scene1/audio/voice/37f802db483c1f2d.mp3",
  "The heat shield protects the bottom of the capsule from the heat of coming back through the air.": "scene1/audio/voice/ed328587c5411fd3.mp3",
  "Mercury Capsule": "scene1/audio/voice/1cad7b2c58995ff7.mp3",
  "This is Freedom 7 itself! It had room for just one astronaut. Astronauts joked that you don't climb into a Mercury capsule, you put it on.": "scene1/audio/voice/d3b57811351294b1.mp3",
  "Parachute Section": "scene1/audio/voice/a67c38651734f2ff.mp3",
  "The top of the capsule holds the parachutes and the radio antennas.": "scene1/audio/voice/db42e95de9c54836.mp3",
  "Launch Escape Tower": "scene1/audio/voice/678f8e4c04b32b3d.mp3",
  "A small rocket on a tower. If something went wrong at launch, it would pull the capsule and the astronaut away to safety.": "scene1/audio/voice/e190211f0a70db0d.mp3",
  "Solar Panels": "scene1/audio/voice/75051df39008239e.mp3",
  "Freedom 7 used batteries. The whole trip lasted only about 15 minutes!": "scene1/audio/voice/a2251bfee74172f6.mp3",
  "Shuttle Wings": "scene1/audio/voice/f7a8c715baf3000f.mp3",
  "Wings came 20 years later with the Space Shuttle. Freedom 7 had no wings.": "scene1/audio/voice/3b179b266649637a.mp3",
  "Landing Legs": "scene1/audio/voice/5b57497f977ba21b.mp3",
  "Freedom 7 splashed down in the ocean, so it didn't need legs to land.": "scene1/audio/voice/deeed13700e3c35e.mp3",
  "Rover Wheels": "scene1/audio/voice/3958b96574a26c53.mp3",
  "Wheels are for rovers that drive on the Moon or Mars. Freedom 7 never needed to drive!": "scene1/audio/voice/b54abdf5f69dcbd1.mp3",
  "Giant Dish Antenna": "scene1/audio/voice/537d0354f84d96bc.mp3",
  "Big dishes talk across huge distances. Freedom 7 stayed close to Earth, so small antennas were enough.": "scene1/audio/voice/29a3bafb9c885e42.mp3",
  "Saturn V Rocket": "scene1/audio/voice/863c9507d75510bc.mp3",
  "The giant Saturn V came later, for trips to the Moon. Freedom 7 only needed the Redstone.": "scene1/audio/voice/bc2c235c83d9506c.mp3",
  "Here it is: Freedom 7 on top of its Redstone rocket! Drag to spin it around. When you're ready, let's launch!": "scene1/audio/voice/e3ce154480c16c2f.mp3",
  "Fly to space!": "scene1/audio/voice/d32d0a77d8b8cefb.mp3",
  "3, 2, 1... Liftoff! Now YOU fly Freedom 7 up through the sky.": "scene1/audio/voice/64f1c80cee787f5f.mp3",
  "Steer left and right to dodge clouds, birds, balloons and meteors. Use the arrow keys, or touch and drag. Climb to 100 kilometers, where space begins!": "scene1/audio/voice/358201c4e3f3fd38.mp3",
  "Altitude": "scene1/audio/voice/d66c27fd7ae6ebd8.mp3",
  "km": "scene1/audio/voice/72573ffeeacc35d8.mp3",
  "Troposphere: this is where all our weather happens!": "scene1/audio/voice/f9ee9e6510dea797.mp3",
  "Stratosphere: the ozone layer up here protects us from the Sun's harsh rays.": "scene1/audio/voice/64cf2539cfe43bf8.mp3",
  "Mesosphere: most meteors burn up here as shooting stars!": "scene1/audio/voice/f4c601ab1d3b70c1.mp3",
  "Thermosphere: the air is super thin. Auroras glow up here.": "scene1/audio/voice/a27c99561f73182b.mp3",
  "You crossed the Kármán line at 100 km. You're in SPACE!": "scene1/audio/voice/cb7c49e0463be077.mp3",
  "Great flying! The real Freedom 7 kept climbing to 187 kilometers (116 miles).": "scene1/audio/voice/f600535794d2c49f.mp3",
  "Alan Shepard floated weightless for about five minutes before falling back to Earth.": "scene1/audio/voice/c6a00582066d8ec0.mp3",
  "You reached space!": "scene1/audio/voice/f802c88cfbd16aa3.mp3",
  "The first photo from space": "scene1/audio/voice/900fd29467a4cebf.mp3",
  "Cameras went to space before people did! In 1946, a camera on a V-2 rocket took the first photo of Earth from space, about 105 km (65 miles) up.": "scene1/audio/voice/01574ca73baf71f4.mp3",
  "Freedom 7's view": "scene1/audio/voice/125986a5d6bf8f7e.mp3",
  "During his flight, Alan Shepard looked at Earth through a periscope. He said: 'What a beautiful view!'": "scene1/audio/voice/3eda9d1ad620ed6c.mp3",
  "Liftoff!": "scene1/audio/voice/c6221d0c3921bbf8.mp3",
  "Here is the real launch of Freedom 7 from Cape Canaveral, Florida, on May 5, 1961.": "scene1/audio/voice/fc8ffa237dedd190.mp3",
  "Mission facts": "scene1/audio/voice/3337ecea988b098a.mp3",
  "Flight time: 15 minutes 28 seconds. Highest point: 187 km (116 miles). Top speed: 8,262 km/h (5,134 mph).": "scene1/audio/voice/36ee2d11461b65af.mp3",
  "Splashdown!": "scene1/audio/voice/de4e4685fdef02ee.mp3",
  "Freedom 7 landed in the Atlantic Ocean. A helicopter picked up Shepard and his capsule and carried them to the ship USS Lake Champlain.": "scene1/audio/voice/09881e6a4849ac09.mp3",
  "What happened next?": "scene1/audio/voice/b18a31532948a5bd.mp3",
  "Ten years later, Alan Shepard walked on the Moon with Apollo 14, and he even hit golf balls there!": "scene1/audio/voice/51e9c8f810abe2ca.mp3",
  "Amazing work, star sailor! Next, let's go back to 1958 and meet America's first satellite: Explorer 1.": "scene1/audio/voice/b6659a5225ab0ca5.mp3",
  "What is a satellite?": "scene2/audio/voice/9ec6271543d86e78.mp3",
  "A satellite is anything that travels around a planet in a loop called an orbit. The Moon is Earth's natural satellite!": "scene2/audio/voice/82df131edaca0a33.mp3",
  "Why doesn't it fall down?": "scene2/audio/voice/4bb011c1180ce5ae.mp3",
  "It does fall! But it also moves sideways so fast, about 28,000 km/h (17,500 mph), that it keeps missing the ground. Isaac Newton imagined this with a cannon on a very tall mountain.": "scene2/audio/voice/c1f25fab12e356ed.mp3",
  "Satellites help us every day": "scene2/audio/voice/9200fc1d45ca82e8.mp3",
  "Today, satellites help us forecast the weather, watch TV, find our way with maps, make phone calls, and look after Earth's forests and oceans.": "scene2/audio/voice/4f49fea89fe9eaf0.mp3",
  "Back to 1958": "scene2/audio/voice/062cf26c88bf92cf.mp3",
  "Let's travel back to 1958, three years before Freedom 7. The Soviet Union had already launched Sputnik, and the United States wanted to launch a satellite too.": "scene2/audio/voice/3f7110c9163537fd.mp3",
  "Meet Explorer 1": "scene2/audio/voice/913cb772070445a5.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 was built at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) in California. JPL became part of NASA later that same year.": "scene2/audio/voice/c910ed8fe586706b.mp3",
  "Launch!": "scene2/audio/voice/81528ac2f9a70d83.mp3",
  "On January 31, 1958, a Juno I rocket launched Explorer 1 from Cape Canaveral, Florida. Here is a NASA film about Explorer 1 and everything that came after it.": "scene2/audio/voice/d3c0faaf08f5f1e8.mp3",
  "A science mission": "scene2/audio/voice/1003a8ba686e188e.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 carried an experiment by Dr. James Van Allen to count tiny, fast particles in space. It was about 2 m (80 in) long and weighed about 14 kg (30.8 lb).": "scene2/audio/voice/4e5b17da9b260f50.mp3",
  "Let's put Explorer 1 together, guide it into orbit, and keep it steady up there!": "scene2/audio/voice/862b42a141548af5.mp3",
  "Oops! That spot is for a different part:": "scene2/audio/voice/6f94de240551bebc.mp3",
  "Here is the blueprint of Explorer 1. Its nose points to the right.": "scene2/audio/voice/04f495840194b374.mp3",
  "Drag each part from the parts box onto the matching shape. I'll tell you what each part does!": "scene2/audio/voice/9584aaaa52eee3a0.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 is complete! A small satellite with a big job: measuring space around Earth.": "scene2/audio/voice/7730de949afc410a.mp3",
  "Rocket Motor": "scene2/audio/voice/b0769291f757eb68.mp3",
  "The last rocket stage. It gave the final push into orbit and stayed attached to Explorer 1.": "scene2/audio/voice/1546c9cc912fffe0.mp3",
  "Whip Antennas": "scene2/audio/voice/7778ef8d8b9fd84a.mp3",
  "Four long, bendy antennas helped send radio signals. Their wobbling later made Explorer 1 tumble!": "scene2/audio/voice/11b0372fa79005f7.mp3",
  "Batteries": "scene2/audio/voice/1f497edac3523940.mp3",
  "Batteries powered the science instruments and radios. They lasted until May 1958.": "scene2/audio/voice/488a1231053bea40.mp3",
  "Radio Transmitters": "scene2/audio/voice/e7c67ee110a63ca0.mp3",
  "Two small radios sent the science readings down to Earth as radio signals.": "scene2/audio/voice/4ac5de503593362a.mp3",
  "Cosmic Ray Detector": "scene2/audio/voice/1b5362011bfebe11.mp3",
  "A Geiger counter that counted tiny, fast particles in space. It found the Van Allen radiation belts!": "scene2/audio/voice/2af2fe4d555e1ee0.mp3",
  "Micrometeorite Detector": "scene2/audio/voice/2c803bd1445cf0e4.mp3",
  "Thin wires and a tiny microphone that noticed hits from specks of space dust.": "scene2/audio/voice/35b3f20830a8a90d.mp3",
  "Nose Cone": "scene2/audio/voice/324e4c0c1ee1387d.mp3",
  "Protects the instruments during launch. Sensors inside measured temperature, and the stripes helped keep it not too hot and not too cold.": "scene2/audio/voice/ae5b904c030fdfc8.mp3",
  "Here is Explorer 1! It spun like a spinning top to stay steady. Drag to spin it around, then let's launch it!": "scene2/audio/voice/dfbb3f6bf8146c38.mp3",
  "Rockets don't fly straight up to reach orbit. They slowly tip over and fly sideways, faster and faster.": "scene2/audio/voice/c24468bf67ccfa11.mp3",
  "Steer the rocket along the glowing path. Use the arrow keys, or touch the screen where you want it to go. Watch the TVs light up as you climb!": "scene2/audio/voice/8d15f4bf8b4075a2.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 is in orbit!": "scene2/audio/voice/ec5578572eda2a01.mp3",
  "Satellites like Explorer 1 started a revolution. The TVs show some of what came next!": "scene2/audio/voice/3517a1a8c7dce7a7.mp3",
  "Explorer 1": "scene2/audio/voice/0d05e72d674eae50.mp3",
  "America's first satellite sends radio beeps and science data.": "scene2/audio/voice/482981347393b1f3.mp3",
  "TIROS-1": "scene2/audio/voice/94118ecca2e71ebe.mp3",
  "The first successful weather satellite sends TV pictures of clouds.": "scene2/audio/voice/900e4afb66459934.mp3",
  "Telstar 1": "scene2/audio/voice/bcb33f41b026f1d7.mp3",
  "The first live TV pictures sent across the Atlantic Ocean by satellite.": "scene2/audio/voice/19131f2600d80385.mp3",
  "Syncom 3": "scene2/audio/voice/34a18719bed5d74a.mp3",
  "A satellite high above one spot helps send the Tokyo Olympics to TVs in America.": "scene2/audio/voice/52ffc4a5ef6ceaaf.mp3",
  "Landsat 1": "scene2/audio/voice/dc2b1715d4fdb371.mp3",
  "The first satellite made to photograph Earth's land. It is still helping scientists today through newer Landsats.": "scene2/audio/voice/86b356dcfc012c33.mp3",
  "GPS": "scene2/audio/voice/af614eace3f4e43c.mp3",
  "The first GPS satellite launches. Today GPS helps maps find where you are.": "scene2/audio/voice/1ceb2eee853c809d.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 had no thrusters. It spun like a pencil to stay steady, but its bendy antennas slowly wasted that spin, and it started to tumble end over end! Engineers learned from this when they designed later satellites.": "scene2/audio/voice/f6820a4e5c4161cf.mp3",
  "Many modern satellites DO have tiny thrusters. Let's pretend Explorer 1 had some! Tap when the white marker is inside the green zone to push the satellite back onto its orbit.": "scene2/audio/voice/e2f3b5d7479f09f0.mp3",
  "Oh no, it drifted too far! Let's bring it back and try again.": "scene2/audio/voice/75c5ecbcca92b8a1.mp3",
  "Steady as can be!": "scene2/audio/voice/6c725fb7468b52ef.mp3",
  "Great timing! Real satellites use small puffs like these to stay exactly where they belong. It's called station keeping.": "scene2/audio/voice/20f9e369edca8431.mp3",
  "A big discovery": "scene2/audio/voice/6d25482ec3099263.mp3",
  "Explorer 1's Geiger counter found belts of fast particles trapped by Earth's magnetism. They are called the Van Allen radiation belts, after Dr. James Van Allen.": "scene2/audio/voice/d1f554c44c5b5f5b.mp3",
  "The first launch photo": "scene2/audio/voice/d88ce90a93b1fd4a.mp3",
  "Here is the real Juno I rocket carrying Explorer 1 into the night sky on January 31, 1958.": "scene2/audio/voice/1cd7979bfeb0a705.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 by the numbers": "scene2/audio/voice/45d2c537ea4cb485.mp3",
  "It sent science data until its batteries ran out in May 1958, but it kept circling Earth until March 31, 1970, more than 58,000 times!": "scene2/audio/voice/d44a9a2d3fb1d6ee.mp3",
  "A whole family of Explorers": "scene2/audio/voice/26e2daf7465968f4.mp3",
  "Explorer 1 was the first of many. NASA has launched many more Explorer missions since, and thousands of satellites now circle our planet.": "scene2/audio/voice/ba2e976e9e693b22.mp3",
  "Brilliant! Now for the biggest adventure of all: Apollo 11, the first time people walked on the Moon!": "scene2/audio/voice/bc80465d1ebea8bf.mp3",
  "A giant goal": "scene3/audio/voice/a327ec906ec30c5f.mp3",
  "In 1961, President John F. Kennedy asked America to land a person on the Moon and bring them home safely before the end of the 1960s.": "scene3/audio/voice/76b5e0ff6c8d59af.mp3",
  "The crew": "scene3/audio/voice/4c381313895b0fc0.mp3",
  "Three astronauts were chosen: Neil Armstrong, the commander; Buzz Aldrin, who would land with him; and Michael Collins, who would fly the spaceship around the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/49f74ffe322194e5.mp3",
  "Two spaceships in one": "scene3/audio/voice/c63d07c35493ad8f.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 had two spaceships: Columbia, where the crew lived, and Eagle, the lander that would touch down on the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/d098f4761cfd2d18.mp3",
  "The biggest rocket": "scene3/audio/voice/20583ee52aac1312.mp3",
  "They rode on a Saturn V rocket, 110.6 meters (363 feet) tall. That's taller than the Statue of Liberty!": "scene3/audio/voice/07df081058ecdcd2.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 highlights": "scene3/audio/voice/c4178054f595c754.mp3",
  "Here is NASA's short film of the whole adventure, from liftoff to leaving the Moon. Now it's your turn to live it!": "scene3/audio/voice/e21ddea0b2df5eaa.mp3",
  "To reach the Moon we need the right parts. The checklist on the left shows exactly what Apollo 11 needs.": "scene3/audio/voice/a22bc612b73c7fb8.mp3",
  "Tap parts to add them to the rocket. You can pick any part you like, but the rocket will only fly with the right ones!": "scene3/audio/voice/144a2aea594b8601.mp3",
  "Almost! You still need:": "scene3/audio/voice/70357587f918de58.mp3",
  "Hold on! These parts would make the mission fail:": "scene3/audio/voice/aa93f81a9c083d43.mp3",
  "Perfect! That's everything Apollo 11 needs. Let's see the whole rocket stacked together!": "scene3/audio/voice/d66b2044a904b8f5.mp3",
  "Could pull the crew to safety if something went wrong during launch.": "scene3/audio/voice/70c65ae607cc72d3.mp3",
  "Jet Engine": "scene3/audio/voice/39706df92959261f.mp3",
  "Jet engines need air to breathe. Rockets carry their own oxygen, so they work in space.": "scene3/audio/voice/c64c65598f67b541.mp3",
  "Command Module 'Columbia'": "scene3/audio/voice/db2dc66f02924972.mp3",
  "Where the three astronauts lived. The only part that came back to Earth.": "scene3/audio/voice/1dca1f92df5d5b2b.mp3",
  "Stage 1 (S-IC)": "scene3/audio/voice/8578b4583448935f.mp3",
  "The biggest stage, with five huge F-1 engines to lift the rocket off the ground.": "scene3/audio/voice/7fc23d99edc1c603.mp3",
  "It only fits one astronaut. Apollo 11 had a crew of three!": "scene3/audio/voice/b062686c8e27bb17.mp3",
  "Service Module": "scene3/audio/voice/dfe0b09b629c79f8.mp3",
  "Held the main engine, fuel, oxygen and power for the trip.": "scene3/audio/voice/a72b736f74106f4c.mp3",
  "Apollo made electricity with fuel cells, which also made drinking water. Solar panels would just add weight.": "scene3/audio/voice/8591297aeb87e94d.mp3",
  "Stage 2 (S-II)": "scene3/audio/voice/4f95e82549aac4d5.mp3",
  "Five J-2 engines pushed Apollo 11 higher and faster, almost to orbit.": "scene3/audio/voice/3d6abc526ffabfda.mp3",
  "Moon Buggy": "scene3/audio/voice/475dce8cce172613.mp3",
  "The Moon buggy first went to the Moon on Apollo 15, in 1971. Apollo 11's astronauts explored on foot.": "scene3/audio/voice/6a48137fc3c25d0d.mp3",
  "Lunar Module 'Eagle'": "scene3/audio/voice/1314305dbf1ffa78.mp3",
  "The lander that carried two astronauts down to the Moon's surface.": "scene3/audio/voice/315b3c8bd32403d4.mp3",
  "The Moon has no air, so wings can't help there. Wings came later, with the Space Shuttle.": "scene3/audio/voice/10886a5c54343afb.mp3",
  "Stage 3 (S-IVB)": "scene3/audio/voice/777b55031ed63e82.mp3",
  "One J-2 engine that fired twice: once to reach orbit, and again to head for the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/96c5c63bd8736075.mp3",
  "Much too weak. It lifted Freedom 7 to space, but the Moon is about 384,000 km away!": "scene3/audio/voice/28981c6e9d784b10.mp3",
  "Instrument Unit": "scene3/audio/voice/d3a320b513a14e44.mp3",
  "The rocket's brain: a ring of computers that steered the Saturn V.": "scene3/audio/voice/6850bd20ee3d7bac.mp3",
  "Here it is: the mighty Saturn V, with Apollo 11 on top! Drag to spin it. Next, let's discover how hard it was to build.": "scene3/audio/voice/5764723e82b9a667.mp3",
  "About 400,000 people worked on Apollo! Let's find out how hard some of the parts were to make.": "scene3/audio/voice/a8acbe1ea01d5599.mp3",
  "Tap each glowing dot on the rocket to read its story. Then choose how hard it was to build. I'll tell you straight away if you're right!": "scene3/audio/voice/a7017df495404b8a.mp3",
  "How hard was it to make?": "scene3/audio/voice/7ad50b8f5f824010.mp3",
  "Correct!": "scene3/audio/voice/dc9a840f28fd197f.mp3",
  "Not quite.": "scene3/audio/voice/9c5c33c2a953abaa.mp3",
  "You found out how hard each part was to make. Every one needed brilliant ideas and a lot of hard work!": "scene3/audio/voice/cec2d92913252fac.mp3",
  "F-1 Rocket Engines": "scene3/audio/voice/a2fd8f7083f05820.mp3",
  "The F-1 is still the most powerful single-nozzle liquid-fuel rocket engine ever flown.": "scene3/audio/voice/2b077e261e9a3662.mp3",
  "Early test engines shook themselves apart. Engineers even set off small bombs inside them until they found a design that stayed steady.": "scene3/audio/voice/899a6eadc0d98579.mp3",
  "Rocket engines already existed. The F-1 made that idea much, much bigger.": "scene3/audio/voice/760fde7b43b023f7.mp3",
  "Rocket engines already existed, so the F-1 was an old idea made much bigger and better. (Still super hard!)": "scene3/audio/voice/f70d72d09ffcc961.mp3",
  "Lunar Module": "scene3/audio/voice/01fb5dfb7082e761.mp3",
  "It was the first crewed spaceship built to fly only in space. It was never meant to fly through Earth's air.": "scene3/audio/voice/d0ed0327bf4b67ba.mp3",
  "To save weight, some of its walls were about as thin as a few sheets of paper.": "scene3/audio/voice/11d97a5b08d96110.mp3",
  "It was test-flown on Apollo 9 and Apollo 10 before it landed on Apollo 11.": "scene3/audio/voice/0bc421b175c6cbc4.mp3",
  "Nobody had ever built a spaceship to land on another world before. It was brand new.": "scene3/audio/voice/1ea657c7bcefcf5c.mp3",
  "Apollo Guidance Computer": "scene3/audio/voice/6ebf1b8596de1602.mp3",
  "It was one of the first computers to use tiny computer chips called integrated circuits.": "scene3/audio/voice/6f27b41240375e28.mp3",
  "Its programs were woven by hand: workers threaded copper wires through or around tiny magnetic rings to store the 1s and 0s.": "scene3/audio/voice/c121d01e28246e6d.mp3",
  "Margaret Hamilton led the team that wrote its flight software. No computer like it had ever flown people to another world.": "scene3/audio/voice/845959077b69b531.mp3",
  "A computer small enough to fly to the Moon had never existed. The team had to invent it.": "scene3/audio/voice/a4c1a419c2bcbb5a.mp3",
  "Coming home, the capsule hit the air at almost 40,000 km/h. The shield faced about 2,760 °C (5,000 °F).": "scene3/audio/voice/7022349905b658b5.mp3",
  "Workers filled a honeycomb of hundreds of thousands of tiny cells with a special material, one cell at a time, by hand.": "scene3/audio/voice/8ba351f3821d784a.mp3",
  "It was made to slowly char and flake away, carrying the heat off with it.": "scene3/audio/voice/408ebf8d5d280ecf.mp3",
  "The heat shield needed a special new material and a huge amount of careful handwork.": "scene3/audio/voice/b36fed4b3df12741.mp3",
  "Spacesuits": "scene3/audio/voice/371261dbae9635cd.mp3",
  "Each Moon suit had about 21 layers to protect against heat, cold and tiny specks of space dust.": "scene3/audio/voice/c226c729430bf413.mp3",
  "After a fire during a ground test in 1967, engineers created a new fireproof fabric called Beta cloth.": "scene3/audio/voice/cd76301d2a51aa89.mp3",
  "Skilled seamstresses sewed the suits by hand, with every stitch in exactly the right place.": "scene3/audio/voice/5a1c952b5370c497.mp3",
  "The suits needed new fireproof materials and very careful sewing by hand.": "scene3/audio/voice/4385d9ff48200419.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 wasn't just three astronauts. Thousands of people on Earth had to talk to each other all the time!": "scene3/audio/voice/3a3aa4596019228c.mp3",
  "Connect the teams the way messages really travelled. Drag a wire from one team to another, or tap one and then the other. Follow the clues on the right.": "scene3/audio/voice/5889381b4dc4dcae.mp3",
  "Every team is connected! This network of people kept Apollo 11 safe from launch to splashdown.": "scene3/audio/voice/1c485b3db2e0313e.mp3",
  "These two teams didn't talk directly. Think about who passes the message along!": "scene3/audio/voice/3eddd662a4a9c5b0.mp3",
  "Radio calls from the astronauts are caught by giant dish antennas on Earth.": "scene3/audio/voice/eb62a88082d6cb9c.mp3",
  "The tracking stations pass the voices and data on to Mission Control.": "scene3/audio/voice/4cab5e78e29c64a6.mp3",
  "Once the rocket clears the tower, Launch Control hands the flight to Mission Control.": "scene3/audio/voice/7f433fc170cb9bfa.mp3",
  "When there's a problem, Mission Control asks engineers and scientists for help.": "scene3/audio/voice/9aa894a670cc5cc7.mp3",
  "Mission Control keeps NASA Headquarters updated.": "scene3/audio/voice/ee5565db1e55b4f0.mp3",
  "NASA Headquarters reports to the President and the government.": "scene3/audio/voice/0863582d3ad9dbac.mp3",
  "Mission Control tells the recovery ship where the capsule will splash down.": "scene3/audio/voice/495b176d76764929.mp3",
  "Mission Control sends the TV pictures on to the networks, so the whole world can watch.": "scene3/audio/voice/27816e175b93ad93.mp3",
  "Close! Radio calls from space are caught by the giant antennas first, then passed to Mission Control.": "scene3/audio/voice/fd6360a9af5702f7.mp3",
  "The President did call the astronauts on the Moon, but the call went through Mission Control and the antennas.": "scene3/audio/voice/4646ebbdb249aa21.mp3",
  "The TV pictures from the Moon went through the antennas and Mission Control first.": "scene3/audio/voice/3b61156bd99dbd03.mp3",
  "The crew talked to Launch Control before liftoff, but in this map we follow the flight after it leaves the pad.": "scene3/audio/voice/0df5abee6226e4e3.mp3",
  "It's launch day: July 16, 1969! Before liftoff, the launch team goes through a long checklist.": "scene3/audio/voice/fb25e4759a0ffa81.mp3",
  "Copy each arrow sequence to finish every step. Use the arrow keys, tap the arrow buttons, or swipe in the right direction.": "scene3/audio/voice/277e5a3b585a4ae6.mp3",
  "Access arm swings away": "scene3/audio/voice/fb9d20e4cb97abc8.mp3",
  "The walkway the astronauts used to climb aboard swings back from the rocket.": "scene3/audio/voice/2e35108fb5ded9e8.mp3",
  "Fuel tanks pressurized": "scene3/audio/voice/79f013ae34befb68.mp3",
  "Gas is pumped into the tanks to push the fuel toward the engines.": "scene3/audio/voice/e9eafb2e107d2d63.mp3",
  "Switch to internal power": "scene3/audio/voice/c59ce19f9ebc98bc.mp3",
  "About 50 seconds before liftoff, the rocket switches to its own batteries.": "scene3/audio/voice/d1215e712426f68b.mp3",
  "Ignition sequence start": "scene3/audio/voice/0dde417eba7f65c8.mp3",
  "About 9 seconds before liftoff, the five F-1 engines start up.": "scene3/audio/voice/8e914bb7dcb8ce62.mp3",
  "Release hold-down arms": "scene3/audio/voice/44fbeb16bb7e6744.mp3",
  "Giant arms hold the rocket down until the engines reach full power. Then they let go!": "scene3/audio/voice/2353963e09641253.mp3",
  "T-0: Release the arms now!": "scene3/audio/voice/8e71813e4dc92e10.mp3",
  "Liftoff! We have liftoff of Apollo 11!": "scene3/audio/voice/4d03eadf9c69a799.mp3",
  "The real liftoff": "scene3/audio/voice/17a8e5632032a80c.mp3",
  "On July 16, 1969, at 9:32 in the morning, Apollo 11 lifted off from Kennedy Space Center in Florida.": "scene3/audio/voice/a4962480bf566095.mp3",
  "NASA footage": "scene3/audio/voice/91cecda1a3f1d209.mp3",
  "Watch NASA's footage of the mission, starting with the launch. The ground shook for miles as the five F-1 engines roared!": "scene3/audio/voice/38c5541cd8dcc031.mp3",
  "Next stop: the Moon": "scene3/audio/voice/b23bd4c99ef2f331.mp3",
  "After less than 3 hours circling Earth, the third stage fired again and sent Apollo 11 on its way to the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/0fbb11528768941d.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 is on its way to the Moon! The trip takes about 3 days.": "scene3/audio/voice/9ecf94a4471951a0.mp3",
  "Move up and down to dodge satellites near Earth, and space rocks and dust further out. Use the arrow keys, or touch and drag.": "scene3/audio/voice/27db9f58ed23924d.mp3",
  "Near Earth, we pass satellites circling our planet.": "scene3/audio/voice/cbd593d40d6f11a6.mp3",
  "The Moon is about 384,000 km (239,000 miles) away.": "scene3/audio/voice/d1d0315a0b25074f.mp3",
  "Tiny specks of space dust zoom by at incredible speeds!": "scene3/audio/voice/08b5e1547c94ef30.mp3",
  "Shield bump!": "scene3/audio/voice/c14b1889af64abee.mp3",
  "You reached the Moon!": "scene3/audio/voice/4e4d3aa12b054e5d.mp3",
  "Now Michael Collins stays in Columbia, circling the Moon, while Neil and Buzz fly Eagle down to the surface.": "scene3/audio/voice/ac79a90d56f5fdce.mp3",
  "This is it! Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin fly Eagle down to the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/fa184bd033784d4d.mp3",
  "Hold THRUST to slow down, and use the side jets to move left and right. Land gently on the flat, safe spot with the green lights. Keep your speeds green!": "scene3/audio/voice/6d1a1a48937b54cb.mp3",
  "Eagle's shadow shows where you will land. If it gets tricky, switch on Landing help and the computer will slow you down for you.": "scene3/audio/voice/6b5a2c1d3a209eba.mp3",
  "Landing help is on! The computer keeps you slow. You just steer to the green lights.": "scene3/audio/voice/51bb77653a98932d.mp3",
  "The real Eagle had a computer to help, too! Want Landing help? It keeps your down speed gentle, so you only need to steer.": "scene3/audio/voice/5e2a8222e13585e9.mp3",
  "Program alarm 1202! The computer has too much to do... Mission Control: 'We're GO on that alarm!'": "scene3/audio/voice/676b03c26fd907ea.mp3",
  "Mission Control: '30 seconds!' Not much fuel left!": "scene3/audio/voice/2ee32666f82b22f0.mp3",
  "Bumpy ground here! Hold THRUST to hop up, then fly over to the green lights.": "scene3/audio/voice/b6648569ab24985e.mp3",
  "The Eagle has landed!": "scene3/audio/voice/028f764670d43242.mp3",
  "Just like the real landing on July 20, 1969! Neil Armstrong flew past a crater full of boulders to find a safe spot, with only seconds of fuel to spare.": "scene3/audio/voice/34698abe41f72e73.mp3",
  "Too fast! Hold THRUST as you get close to the ground.": "scene3/audio/voice/a818294bb9532ce9.mp3",
  "Sliding sideways too fast! Stop drifting before you touch down.": "scene3/audio/voice/af3a1d83eb7a4aee.mp3",
  "Those are boulders! Aim for the flat, safe spot.": "scene3/audio/voice/31b643eeed68ae3d.mp3",
  "Out of fuel! Try using thrust in short bursts.": "scene3/audio/voice/222f454da76ef676.mp3",
  "\"The Eagle has landed.\"": "scene3/audio/voice/6dc2da00c8507e3b.mp3",
  "This NASA film was shot through Eagle's window as it came down. After touchdown, Neil Armstrong radioed: 'Houston, Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed.'": "scene3/audio/voice/ac4b303454df165e.mp3",
  "One small step": "scene3/audio/voice/1d1f1571c1a0e74d.mp3",
  "Armstrong stepped onto the Moon and said: 'That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.' Watch NASA's footage of the moonwalk!": "scene3/audio/voice/0414fd2db906980a.mp3",
  "Armstrong stepped onto the Moon and said: That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind. Watch NASA's footage of the moonwalk!": "scene3/audio/voice/7cdc6e40b0ed49f7.mp3",
  "Planting the flag": "scene3/audio/voice/5d9009f2e76faae2.mp3",
  "Buzz Aldrin joined Neil on the surface. Together they set up a flag and science experiments, and collected Moon rocks.": "scene3/audio/voice/cdf660bb2b7b836b.mp3",
  "Scientists back on Earth were waiting to study real Moon rocks!": "scene3/audio/voice/2b7568c655c3ef4f.mp3",
  "First, tap each item on the ground to collect it. Then we'll sort everything into the right boxes for the scientists.": "scene3/audio/voice/0bd3622f15b83921.mp3",
  "Great collecting! Now drag each item into the right box. Look closely at the rocks!": "scene3/audio/voice/4fe10cb37f381114.mp3",
  "Not that box.": "scene3/audio/voice/8176781f32088efb.mp3",
  "Try this box instead:": "scene3/audio/voice/84bda5ee458ce96d.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 brought back 21.5 kg (47.5 pounds) of Moon rocks and soil. The crew also left science behind: a seismometer to feel moonquakes, and a laser mirror that scientists still bounce lasers off today!": "scene3/audio/voice/8f92a7b6794e0e55.mp3",
  "Lava rocks": "scene3/audio/voice/4a87856c8ffe1f6b.mp3",
  "Jumbled rocks": "scene3/audio/voice/f3739e8b27384935.mp3",
  "Moon soil": "scene3/audio/voice/b1d2f02e46dbe6c8.mp3",
  "Science data": "scene3/audio/voice/185421d2678c36a5.mp3",
  "First soil sample": "scene3/audio/voice/3bdb004f5f014693.mp3",
  "Neil Armstrong scooped this up first, just in case they had to leave in a hurry.": "scene3/audio/voice/547df2fa0b7265c4.mp3",
  "Dark rock": "scene3/audio/voice/767fd8a64be64205.mp3",
  "Basalt formed from lava. The tiny holes were gas bubbles!": "scene3/audio/voice/ee000ecfaa4b0153.mp3",
  "Speckled rock": "scene3/audio/voice/c9df9fce137a93e1.mp3",
  "Breccia is broken rock glued together when space rocks crashed into the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/62141a433cb577bb.mp3",
  "Solar wind foil": "scene3/audio/voice/fb97fdfcaec98c75.mp3",
  "This foil caught tiny particles from the Sun. It was rolled up and brought back to Earth!": "scene3/audio/voice/82c10a75410795b4.mp3",
  "The dark 'seas' of the Moon are made of old lava rock like this.": "scene3/audio/voice/1bbac8e8e220e1ba.mp3",
  "Core tube": "scene3/audio/voice/74b667c322789d3d.mp3",
  "Tubes hammered into the ground collected layers of Moon soil.": "scene3/audio/voice/7a2dd57769ff4a41.mp3",
  "Breccias tell the story of billions of years of crashes on the Moon.": "scene3/audio/voice/444863d5d6315ef6.mp3",
  "Camera film": "scene3/audio/voice/437ebc7e37fd700f.mp3",
  "Film from the Moon cameras. It held the famous photos of the moonwalk!": "scene3/audio/voice/9030445f9e597372.mp3",
  "Some Apollo 11 basalts are about 3.6 to 3.9 billion years old.": "scene3/audio/voice/4ee5dbe3fee92e5b.mp3",
  "Bag of Moon dust": "scene3/audio/voice/e7be90409218a999.mp3",
  "Moon dust is powdery and sticky. It clung to the astronauts' suits!": "scene3/audio/voice/48e91f9361332ef1.mp3",
  "Back inside Eagle, Buzz Aldrin spotted a problem: a switch had broken off! Without it, the engine that lifts them off the Moon might not start.": "scene3/audio/voice/74046e86968167a1.mp3",
  "Drag the right tool onto the broken switch. Think carefully: there's electricity inside!": "scene3/audio/voice/86a927158c0e5e37.mp3",
  "Metal carries electricity. It could cause a spark inside the switch!": "scene3/audio/voice/3eb54904b331d940.mp3",
  "Too rough! You could break the whole panel.": "scene3/audio/voice/11c5163081e7b307.mp3",
  "Tape can't push the switch in. It needs to be pressed firmly.": "scene3/audio/voice/4f91dd9ed00cfef2.mp3",
  "It worked! Buzz Aldrin really used a felt-tip pen, because it wasn't made of metal. Eagle lifted off the Moon and met up with Columbia.": "scene3/audio/voice/1eb59918e939eb93.mp3",
  "Almost home! Before hitting Earth's air, the crew leaves the Service Module behind. Only the Command Module has a heat shield. Hold the button to separate!": "scene3/audio/voice/a041d044f8149500.mp3",
  "Separated! Now only Columbia, the Command Module, is heading home.": "scene3/audio/voice/3b0a036639373f36.mp3",
  "The heat shield must face forward, into the air. Turn the capsule until the shield points at the arrow.": "scene3/audio/voice/01abdf0d001a8452.mp3",
  "Shield forward! That's where the heat will hit.": "scene3/audio/voice/2b2e964520781e2d.mp3",
  "Now choose the entry angle. Too steep and it gets too hot. Too shallow and the capsule skips off the air like a stone on water! Slide into the green zone.": "scene3/audio/voice/a3348c1c6af8da2f.mp3",
  "Too steep! Too hot and too bumpy.": "scene3/audio/voice/eca260f159a7733a.mp3",
  "Too shallow! It would skip off the air.": "scene3/audio/voice/e7221092c23e03b6.mp3",
  "Just right!": "scene3/audio/voice/c1fc673eb8de335e.mp3",
  "Columbia is lined up perfectly for re-entry. Here we go!": "scene3/audio/voice/d71eb3d61404e2a8.mp3",
  "Re-entry!": "scene3/audio/voice/01b0cac8020723e1.mp3",
  "Columbia hit Earth's air at almost 40,000 km/h (25,000 mph). The air in front of it glowed, and the heat shield reached about 2,760 °C (5,000 °F), but the crew inside stayed safe.": "scene3/audio/voice/7b921b7bbe51fe94.mp3",
  "Here is NASA's 1969 film of Apollo 11 coming home: the recovery ship waiting at dawn, the glowing capsule streaking across the sky, and sailors watching for it.": "scene3/audio/voice/87bd8734c8c2dbe3.mp3",
  "Blackout": "scene3/audio/voice/5d3e2f267ebe0899.mp3",
  "For a few minutes, the super-hot air around the capsule blocked the radio. Mission Control waited to hear the crew's voices again.": "scene3/audio/voice/65c068c47a271f18.mp3",
  "Parachutes next!": "scene3/audio/voice/c3bde1a53e0f66d0.mp3",
  "Once the capsule slowed down, it was time for the parachutes. Get ready to help!": "scene3/audio/voice/4355e2aa10af9d4a.mp3",
  "Columbia is falling through the sky. You're in charge of the parachutes and the safety gear!": "scene3/audio/voice/54233b92d7a26383.mp3",
  "Press the button when the height meter reaches the green zone. First the small drogue parachutes, then the three big main parachutes.": "scene3/audio/voice/db5aa182d9f410a1.mp3",
  "Too early! Wait for the green zone.": "scene3/audio/voice/5a7f4cf24c618a5e.mp3",
  "The automatic backup opened them for you. Try to be quicker next time!": "scene3/audio/voice/58e7b783e87f6219.mp3",
  "Drogue chutes open! They steady the capsule and slow it down.": "scene3/audio/voice/f6ab2eb3b79917e7.mp3",
  "Three big main parachutes! Now we float down gently.": "scene3/audio/voice/3dd0403be1dc6c04.mp3",
  "Splashdown in the Pacific Ocean! But the capsule landed upside down, just like the real Apollo 11. Tap the three balloon bags to flip it upright!": "scene3/audio/voice/2ecd28b0fc8d03cf.mp3",
  "Upright! The balloon bags turned the capsule the right way up.": "scene3/audio/voice/d7e0f9a25b7c0671.mp3",
  "Now switch on the safety gear so the rescue team can find us. Tap each one!": "scene3/audio/voice/880a748c018e7b16.mp3",
  "Cut parachutes loose": "scene3/audio/voice/148d1d4b05e30577.mp3",
  "So the wind can't drag the capsule across the waves.": "scene3/audio/voice/63acf5d2a6a5e544.mp3",
  "Flashing light": "scene3/audio/voice/d2719f85182162da.mp3",
  "A bright flashing light helps rescuers see the capsule.": "scene3/audio/voice/08b8c95b5a649676.mp3",
  "Radio beacon": "scene3/audio/voice/297304f5d77cc59d.mp3",
  "It beeps a radio signal that helicopters can follow.": "scene3/audio/voice/00e9f45c591a6dd2.mp3",
  "Green dye marker": "scene3/audio/voice/4b0b916531bf0d99.mp3",
  "Bright green dye spreads in the water, easy to spot from the air.": "scene3/audio/voice/50f776585b20b824.mp3",
  "All safety gear is on. Now let's switch places and help the rescue team find the crew!": "scene3/audio/voice/1c7aa170ef5564d7.mp3",
  "You are flying the recovery helicopter, called Helicopter 66, from the ship USS Hornet.": "scene3/audio/voice/c8816bba63f00ea8.mp3",
  "Follow the radio beeps: the faster they beep, the closer you are. The arrow points toward the signal. Use the arrow keys, or touch where you want to fly.": "scene3/audio/voice/61dff79aafb4d6e2.mp3",
  "Found them! Swimmers jump in to help.": "scene3/audio/voice/114b4f6a8c78f9bd.mp3",
  "Swimmers attach a floating collar around the capsule.": "scene3/audio/voice/4e62c8f747663c0b.mp3",
  "The astronauts put on special suits in case they brought back Moon germs.": "scene3/audio/voice/11895122211bb2ce.mp3",
  "The helicopter lifts the crew up in a basket.": "scene3/audio/voice/76360bb91e15ea3d.mp3",
  "Welcome home! The crew landed on the USS Hornet, where President Nixon was waiting to greet them. Then they spent about three weeks in quarantine, just in case.": "scene3/audio/voice/923c26cb3a48b807.mp3",
  "Mission report: Apollo 11": "scene3/audio/voice/bd134f577cb729cf.mp3",
  "Apollo 11 launched on July 16, 1969, landed on the Moon on July 20, and splashed down safely on July 24. The whole mission took just over 8 days.": "scene3/audio/voice/bdf33acd53997abb.mp3",
  "The steps that led to the Moon": "scene3/audio/voice/92179f136b7ea6c4.mp3",
  "Each mission taught NASA something new: from the first satellites, to flying people in space, to practicing spacewalks and docking, and finally flying around the Moon. Step by step, they got ready for Apollo 11.": "scene3/audio/voice/6d5ecf5caadbf568.mp3",
  "What Apollo gave the world": "scene3/audio/voice/5952e53a298d4d83.mp3",
  "Apollo's hard work still helps us today: better computer chips, careful software, cordless tools, and science that continues more than fifty years later.": "scene3/audio/voice/76290c80ff205225.mp3",
  "You did it, star sailor! From the very first satellite all the way to the Moon. More adventures are coming soon: Apollo 13, Voyager, Mars rovers and the James Webb Space Telescope!": "scene3/audio/voice/6ec9bba65004dbf8.mp3",
  "Read aloud is on.": "voice/0e8f111fe07ab1b3.mp3",
  "This is how fast I will talk.": "voice/9b6bdf3893e9c9e6.mp3",
  "You finished every chapter. Amazing work, star sailor!": "voice/741a6163656cc153.mp3",
  "Welcome home!": "voice/69e62fb93d8a8a67.mp3"
 }
}